    proofTitle:"A decision framework before a delivery claim", proofBody:"The modernization insight explains how business criticality, technical constraints, integration needs, and migration risk should shape the path. Client-specific modernization results will only be published when approved evidence exists.", proofTo:"/insights/application-modernization-refactor-replatform-rebuild-replace", proofLink:"Use the modernize-or-replace framework",
    faqTitle:"Questions that determine the safest modernization path", faq:[["Does modernization mean rewriting the application?","No. Modernization can include remediation, refactoring, replatforming, integration, selective replacement, or retirement."],["How do we decide what to modernize first?","Prioritize capabilities where business importance and change constraint are both high, while accounting for dependencies and reversibility."],["Can old and new components run together?","Often. Coexistence can reduce cutover risk when interfaces, data ownership, synchronization, and retirement criteria are designed explicitly."],["How do you protect undocumented business rules?","Use code, data, user interviews, reports, examples, and tests to identify behavior before changing the capability that contains it."],["Is moving to cloud automatically modernization?","No. A hosting move may help infrastructure operations, but it does not by itself improve architecture, maintainability, or workflow fit."],["What should a modernization roadmap contain?","Treatments by capability, dependencies, target boundaries, migration and validation approach, continuity controls, costs to investigate, and clear decision gates."],],
